course: dictionary
lesson: 11675
title: A mixed bag of words
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Assortment is a noun. It means a mixture of different kinds of things. «Mum bought an assortment of biscuits: chocolate, ginger and jam.» The clues are the word of and the list that follows. Chocolate, ginger and jam are different kinds of biscuit, all mixed together, so an assortment is a mixture.
explain: Look for signs of mixing, such as wide, different, various or all together. «The box held a wide assortment of shapes: circles, stars and triangles.» The word wide tells you there are many different kinds.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it in. Replace assortment with "a mixture of different kinds": «an assortment of tools» becomes «a mixture of different kinds of tools». It still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
fact: Assortment means a mixture of different kinds of things.
fact: «An assortment of» is followed by things that are different from each other.
fact: «A wide assortment» means there are many different kinds.
fact: Assortment can be used for sweets, shapes, tools and people.
fact: In a maths problem, an assortment of coloured beads may come before a probability question.
exercise: «The shop sold an assortment of pens, pencils and rulers.» What does assortment mean? | A single kind of thing | An empty box | A very long line | *A mixture of different kinds of things
exercise: «Dad made a wide assortment of sandwiches for the picnic.» Find and copy the word that tells you there are many kinds. | sandwiches | made | picnic | *wide
exercise: «The bag held an assortment of coloured beads: red, blue, green and yellow.» How many different colours are there in the assortment? | Two | One | *Four | Ten
test: «The toy box held an assortment of cars, dolls and bricks.» What is an assortment? | *A mixture of different kinds of toys | A pile of only cars | A kind of toy | An empty box
test: Which phrase shows that there are lots of different kinds? | a tiny mistake | a single pencil | *a wide assortment | one red ball
exam: Which word is closest in meaning to assortment? | quiet | single | *mixture | empty
